In a system of particles, the force on the $i$-th particle splits as
$
  vb(F_i) = vb(F_i)^"ext" + sum_(j != i) vb(F_(i j)),
$
and the third law makes the internal contributions cancel in pairs:
$
  sum_i sum_(j != i) vb(F_(i j)) = sum_(i < j) (vb(F_(i j)) + vb(F_(j i))) = vb(0).
$
Hence $vb(dot(P)) = sum_i vb(F_i)^"ext"$, so the total momentum of a system is conserved in the absence of external forces. When internal forces are derived from pair potentials $V_(i j)$, the third law also requires $V_(i j) = V_(j i)$.
